{
  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero evidence about which modality, encoder, profile setting, or aggregation strategy is most informative. The entire design space is unexplored. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest, most 'null' RNA configuration. It establishes the baseline performance of RNA data with a standard, non-learned dimensionality reduction (PCA) and the simplest profile setting. This gives a clear reference point to later isolate the effect of learned encoders (scvi, scgpt), multiprofile aggregation, image modality, and fusion. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the first experiment should be fast, interpretable, and maximally informative as a reference. PCA is computationally cheap and introduces no learned-representation confound, singleprofile eliminates the aggregation variable, and rna_only isolates the RNA signal. A strong result confirms RNA carries useful signal and sets a floor; a weak result immediately redirects attention toward image or multimodal configurations. This single experiment reduces the most uncertainty per unit of compute and provides the anchor against which every subsequent comparison (encoder choice, profile setting, modality, fusion) can be measured."
}

